  "response": "Adjudicating between a monetary-stimulus account of the 2021-2023 price surge and a supply-disruption account begins from a simple premise: at least one of two conditions held in that period, and perhaps both, namely that aggregate demand was lifted above pre-pandemic trend by fiscal transfers and low policy rates, and that pandemic-era production and shipping networks were physically disrupted. The scale of the episode is a separate matter of measurement. US headline CPI inflation reached 9.1 percent year over year in June 2022, which shows that headline CPI inflation peaked below 10 percent during the 2021-2023 episode. Either that peak stayed under the double-digit threshold or headline CPI inflation peaked at or above 10 percent during the episode: one of those two holds, and not the other.\n\nThe demand-side case rests on the size and the timing of the stimulus. The American Rescue Plan Act of March 2021 authorized roughly $1.9 trillion in fiscal support, and that transfer of purchasing power onto household balance sheets drove employers' demand for workers far beyond the pool of available labor. The ratio of posted job vacancies to unemployed workers in the United States reached about two to one in March 2022. Bernanke and Blanchard, writing on the episode, attributed the initial 2021 inflation surge primarily to an overheated labor market. Any attribution of the first leg of the surge primarily to labor-market overheating cannot be squared with the conclusion the two authors reached, which was that commodity price shocks and sectoral shortages, rather than labor market tightness, dominated the initial rise in inflation.\n\nRelative prices supply the discriminating test, since a monetary impulse lifts prices across the board while a physical bottleneck lifts them in the specific goods that have become scarce. US used vehicle prices rose about 45 percent year over year in June 2021. The Federal Reserve's first policy rate increase of the cycle came in September 2021. The price movement in that market was concentrated in a single shortage-hit category rather than spread evenly across the consumption basket, which is the signature of a quantity constraint on supply.\n\nBecause spot container shipping rates on Asia-to-US routes rose more than fivefold between early 2020 and late 2021, the freight market itself indicates that the New York Fed's Global Supply Chain Pressure Index reached its record peak in December 2021. That record reading in global supply-chain pressure confirms the verdict of the Bernanke-Blanchard study, because the physical constraint the index measures bound most tightly in the very quarters when consumer prices first accelerated. As those pressures unwound from the December 2021 peak, the consequence was that US core PCE inflation declined substantially during 2023 with the unemployment rate remaining near 4 percent. A disinflation of that shape is what settles the dispute: an overheating account requires slack to be created before price growth subsides, while a bottleneck account predicts exactly the costless unwinding that the data record.",
